# Sprint 2 Exam - the paired half

Week 10 - Wednesday 28 October, in class - 100 points - Sprint 2 Exam

## Purpose

Learn how to ask questions to understand others work and help them deepen their understanding, and write down what you learned.

## Tasks

You have your partner's paper. Complete the tasks below in order. When you finish, press **Copy my answers** and paste the text into the Canvas box.

### Task 1

Select your name and your partner's name.

*Field:* <<To Alan: Not needed>> Your name -> a text box with the course roster as suggestions ("Firstname L.").
*Field:* Your partner's name -> the same list. A name that is not on the list can still be typed.

### Task 2

Type your partner's four handwritten answers into the four boxes below, exactly as they wrote them. Read each box back to your partner and have them tick **Checked by the writer**. If you cannot read something, ask them.

Each box shows the question from the paper above it.

*Box 1 label:* **1a. Explain the quantitative relationship you learned, and how it is helpful to know it.**
*Box 2 label:* **1b. Apply it to one scenario to make a prediction, and explain each step.**
*Box 3 label:* **1c. Present two scenarios that look similar but where the relationship does not apply, and explain why in each case.**
*Box 4 label:* **2. What did you learn about your own learning practice, and how will you put it into practice in another course?**

*Under each box:* a tick box labelled **Checked by the writer** with the line *My partner has read this box against their paper and confirms it says what they wrote.*

*Beside each box:* **Read it aloud** button. Press it, read the paper aloud, and the words land in the box. It becomes **Pause** while listening and **Continue** after a pause. The box stays an ordinary text box, so you edit by hand afterwards.
*Status line beside the button:* Listening - read the paper aloud. Press Pause to ask your partner about a word. / Paused - fix anything in the box, then press Continue. / Not available in this browser - use Chrome, Edge or Safari, or type. / The microphone is blocked. Allow it in the address bar, or open the full page in its own tab, or type. / Listening stopped. Press Continue to try again, or type.
*Hover note on the (?) beside the button:* the same note as the Sprint 1 paired page - what the button does, which browsers it works in, where the audio goes, why it is blocked inside Canvas, and the reminder to check names, numbers and symbols against the paper.

### Task 3

Ask your partner at least two follow-up questions about what they wrote. Write down each question you asked and what your partner said.

A follow-up question that teaches you something points at one specific thing in their answer - a number, a step in the working, the condition they named, a scenario they chose, a word they used. A general question could be asked of any paper in the room, so the answer tells you nothing about this paper.

*Worked pair, shown on the page:*
- General: "Was your topic hard to learn?"
- Specific: "In 1b you divided by the mass at the third step. What would your prediction have been if the mass were twice as large?"

Come up with your questions in either of these two ways, or both.

**Route 1 (+): Write your own questions**

1. Read your partner's four answers again.
2. Write down every question that comes to you. Do not stop to judge or answer them. If you write a statement, change it into a question. Truly care about the questions.
3. For each question you wrote, find the exact words or phrases in their answer that it points at. Cross out any question you cannot point at.
4. Pick the two questions you are most curious about. Ask those two.

*Box label:* **The questions I wrote down**

**Route 2 (+): Use the AI Question Suggestions button**

Press the button. It reads the four answers you typed and suggests five questions, each one naming the part of the answer it comes from. Pick two and adapt to ask your partner - again your curiosity is your guide. Change the wording to make it your own.

*Button:* AI Question Suggestions. Greyed until all four boxes have text and all four **Checked by the writer** ticks are on. *Line beside it while greyed:* Type all four answers and have your partner tick each one, then the suggestions open.
*What it does:* reads the four typed answers and returns five questions. Each question quotes the words in the answer it comes from, and asks about that. It does not suggest questions about the topic in general, about how hard the work was, or about anything that is not in the four boxes.
*If the call fails:* AI Question Suggestions is not available right now. Use "Write your own questions" above.

*Fields:* Follow-up question 1 / What I learned from my partner's response - Follow-up question 2 / What I learned from my partner's response - Follow-up question 3 (if you asked one) / What I learned from my partner's response

### Task 4

Write a summary of what you understand about their work. Include what they could explain when you asked, what they were less sure about, and identify at least one thing you learned from their answers that was not on the paper.

*Box label:* **Your summary**
*Line under the label:* <<To Alan: Why do we have this line here?>> Write what your partner said. Do not judge whether they are right.

## Self-check

Tick each line your submission meets. Your ticks are copied with your answers; the grader verifies each one.

- [ ] Both names are selected · 5 pts
- [ ] All four answers are typed as my partner wrote them, not summarised · 15 pts
- [ ] All four boxes carry my partner's **Checked by the writer** tick · 10 pts << Lets make this one checkbox at the end of all four answers - and reduce the points to 5 points>>
- [ ] I asked at least two follow-up questions, and each one is written down · 15 pts
- [ ] Each follow-up question points at specific parts in one of the four answers, and I am communicate that in my question· 20 pts
- [ ] No follow-up question is generic that you could ask anyone· 5 pts
- [ ] What I learned from my partner's responses is capture for each questions· 15 pts
- [ ] The summary says what my partner could explain and what they were less sure about, · 15 pts

**What makes it strong.** Your two questions explore the part of the paper that is interesting or relevant - the step in 1b where the number comes out, or the condition in 1c that the look-alike scenario breaks - and your summary is traceable to what your partner said when you asked, rather than repeating the paper back.

**Grading.** 100 points, the paired half of the Sprint 2 Exam. You will be asked to explain this to a peer, the TA and/or the instructor, so make sure you are comfortable with what you have written here.

